 "labels": [],
 "sections": [
  {
   "head": "What the graph shows",
   "text": "Earth's average surface temperature over the last 485 million years swung between about 11 °C and 36 °C, far wider than anything people have known (about 14 °C before industry). It peaked in the Cretaceous hothouse and in the brief heat spike 56 million years ago, and bottomed out in the ice ages of the late Carboniferous and of the last few million years. Carbon dioxide in the air rose and fell with it.",
   "sources": [
    "judd2024"
   ]
  },
  {
   "head": "How it is known",
   "text": "Shells and teeth record the temperature of the water they grew in, in their oxygen isotopes and magnesium content, and some fossil molecules do too. PhanDA blends these measurements with climate-model runs for each geological stage, the same data-assimilation method weather forecasts use. Its CO₂ curve is a compilation of separate CO₂ proxies.",
   "sources": [
    "judd2024"
   ]
  },
  {
   "head": "How sure",
   "text": "Each value is the middle of a spread (the grey band holds 90% of the estimates). Data are dense in the Cenozoic, where PhanDA agrees with other methods, and sparse in the early Paleozoic. CO₂ tracks temperature closely: over geological time each doubling went with about 8 °C of warming, with mid-Cretaceous CO₂ the least certain.",
   "sources": [
    "judd2024"
   ]
  },
  {
   "head": "Glacial cycles",
   "text": "The zoom shows the last million years from 57 deep-sea cores: ice sheets grew slowly and collapsed quickly about every 100,000 years, a sawtooth paced by Earth's orbit.",
   "sources": [
    "lisiecki2005"
   ]
  }
 ]
}